WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.495
For years the rule was simple.

00:00:05.495 --> 00:00:08.989
Outgrow Postgres, drop SQL, go NoSQL.

00:00:08.989 --> 00:00:12.483
Then SQL caught up.

00:00:12.483 --> 00:00:15.978
Today one table holds rigid columns, flexible JSON, and AI vectors at once.

00:00:15.978 --> 00:00:19.964
A standard column, a JSON blob, and a vector, all for one user.

00:00:19.964 --> 00:00:23.950
Three databases?

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:27.936
No.

00:00:27.936 --> 00:00:31.922
One Postgres table, three worlds in a single row.

00:00:31.922 --> 00:00:35.908
A rigid user ID, a flexible preferences blob in JSON, and an AI taste profile as a vector, side by side.

00:00:35.908 --> 00:00:39.553
So when do you actually leave SQL?

00:00:39.553 --> 00:00:43.199
Only for the narrow twenty percent.

00:00:43.199 --> 00:00:46.845
Sub-millisecond key-value lookups, or graph traversals like friend-of-a-friend.

00:00:46.845 --> 00:00:50.490
Start with SQL, and specialize when scale truly demands it.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:53.230
So the rule flipped.

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:55.970
Start on SQL, lean on JSON and vectors, and reach for a specialized store only when you must.

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:58.710
Next, the clearest specialized case.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:01.450
Key-value.
